# Metals and Non-Metals: Properties, Reactivity, and Metallurgy

> Three-quarters of the elements are metals, and their reactivity
> ladder governs everything from salt formation to pulling iron out
> of ore.

## 1. Metals vs Non-Metals

| Property | Metals | Non-metals |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Lustre | shiny | dull (iodine is the lustrous exception) |
| State at room temperature | solid (mercury pours as a liquid) | gases or solids (bromine is liquid) |
| Malleability | malleable and ductile | brittle |
| Conduction | conduct heat and electricity | insulators (graphite is the exception) |
| Sonority | ring when struck | silent |
| Oxides | mostly basic | mostly acidic (CO₂, SO₂) |

[warning]
Exam-favourite exceptions: graphite (a non-metal) conducts
electricity; iodine shines; mercury is liquid; sodium is soft
enough to cut with a knife; diamond conducts heat superbly.
[/warning]

## 2. The Reactivity Series

K > Na > Ca > Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Pb > (H) > Cu > Hg > Ag > Au

[tip]
Memory sentence — *Kind Nature Can Make A Zebra Feel Proud; Her
Cute Hamster Always Agrees.* Each initial gives one element, from
potassium down to gold.
[/tip]

| Series position | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| K, Na, Ca | react with cold water → hydroxide + H₂ |
| Mg, Al, Zn, Fe | react with steam → oxide + H₂ |
| above hydrogen | displace H₂ from dilute acids |
| Cu, Hg, Ag, Au | unaffected by water or dilute acids |

## 3. Ionic Compounds and Amphoteric Oxides

Metals hand electrons to non-metals, giving hard, high-melting
ionic salts — NaCl, MgCl₂, CaO.

[definition]
**Amphoteric oxides** (Al₂O₃, ZnO, PbO) act as bases toward strong
acids and as acids toward strong alkalis — they dissolve in both.
[/definition]

## 4. Extracting Metals: Metallurgy

@diagram tree id="metallurgy-flow" layout="tree" aria="Metallurgy steps from ore to refined metal"
  Ore -> "Concentration (remove gangue)"
  "Concentration (remove gangue)" -> "Calcination or roasting"
  "Calcination or roasting" -> "Metal oxide"
  "Metal oxide" -> "Reduction to crude metal"
  "Reduction to crude metal" -> "Refining"
@/diagram

| Step | What happens | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Concentration | gangue removed | froth flotation (sulphide ores), gravity, magnetic separation |
| Calcination | carbonate heated in limited air | ZnCO₃ → ZnO + CO₂ |
| Roasting | sulphide heated in excess air | 2ZnS + 3O₂ → 2ZnO + 2SO₂ |
| Reduction | oxide loses oxygen | carbon in the blast furnace; thermite (Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → 2Fe + Al₂O₃); electrolysis for Al and Na |
| Refining | purification | electrolytic: pure cathode, impure anode, salt electrolyte |

[important]
The more reactive the metal, the harder the extraction: K, Na, Ca,
Mg, and Al need electrolysis; Zn, Fe, and Pb yield to carbon;
Cu, Ag, and Au sometimes occur free in the ground.
[/important]

## 5. Alloys

[definition]
An **alloy** is a homogeneous mixture of a metal with other metals
or non-metals, made to boost hardness, strength, or corrosion
resistance.
[/definition]

| Alloy | Composition | Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Steel | iron + about 1% carbon | construction girders |
| Stainless steel | Fe + Cr + Ni | cutlery; chromium blocks rust |
| Brass | Cu + Zn | fittings, musical instruments |
| Bronze | Cu + Sn | statues, coins |
| Solder | Pb + Sn | low-melting joins in electronics |

## 6. Corrosion and Its Prevention

Corrosion is a metal being oxidized by air and moisture — rust on
iron, black Ag₂S on silver, green CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ on copper.

| Prevention | How it works |
| --- | --- |
| Galvanization | zinc coat on iron; even scratched zinc keeps protecting |
| Alloying | stainless steel resists rust outright |
| Barriers | paint, grease, plastic dip — keep air and water off |
| Sacrificial blocks | more reactive metal corrodes in place (details in the electrochemistry lesson) |

## 7. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Liquid metal? | mercury |
| Conducting non-metal? | graphite |
| Soft metal cut with a knife? | sodium |
| Displaces Cu from CuSO₄? | any metal above Cu — e.g. Fe |
| Sulphide ore concentration? | froth flotation |
| Brass? | Cu + Zn |
| Amphoteric oxides? | Al₂O₃, ZnO |
